1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,611
A script is just commands in a file.

2
00:00:02,611 --> 00:00:04,077
But before you write one,

3
00:00:04,077 --> 00:00:06,832
you must understand how the shell reads a line,

4
00:00:06,832 --> 00:00:09,353
because most script bugs start right there.

5
00:00:09,903 --> 00:00:10,316
Today,

6
00:00:10,316 --> 00:00:11,072
four ideas.

7
00:00:11,072 --> 00:00:12,310
How a script runs.

8
00:00:12,310 --> 00:00:14,442
How quoting protects your text.

9
00:00:14,442 --> 00:00:18,705
How expansion changes your line before a command ever sees it.

10
00:00:18,705 --> 00:00:23,450
And how every command reports success or failure with an exit status.

11
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,075
Every demo is a real recording on Ubuntu, so you can follow along.

12
00:00:30,125 --> 00:00:35,053
First, the big picture. When you press Enter, the shell does not simply run your text.

13
00:00:35,603 --> 00:00:38,099
Step one: it splits the line into words.

14
00:00:38,649 --> 00:00:40,122
Step two: it expands.

15
00:00:40,122 --> 00:00:40,823
Variables,

16
00:00:40,823 --> 00:00:42,366
command substitutions,

17
00:00:42,366 --> 00:00:43,067
wildcards,

18
00:00:43,067 --> 00:00:45,241
and the tilde are all replaced.

19
00:00:45,791 --> 00:00:49,204
Step three: it runs the command, with the expanded words.

20
00:00:49,754 --> 00:00:55,536
Step four: the command finishes, and leaves an exit status. A number. Zero means success.

21
00:00:56,086 --> 00:01:01,248
Keep this order in mind. Almost every strange result comes from step one or step two.

22
00:01:03,298 --> 00:01:05,377
First: how a script runs.

23
00:01:05,377 --> 00:01:08,370
A script is a text file of commands.

24
00:01:08,370 --> 00:01:09,367
Here is one,

25
00:01:09,367 --> 00:01:10,864
called hi dot S H.

26
00:01:10,864 --> 00:01:12,194
Type: cat hi.sh.

27
00:01:12,744 --> 00:01:13,845
The first line,

28
00:01:13,845 --> 00:01:14,211
hash,

29
00:01:14,211 --> 00:01:14,578
bang,

30
00:01:14,578 --> 00:01:16,119
slash bin slash bash,

31
00:01:16,119 --> 00:01:17,733
is called the shebang.

32
00:01:17,733 --> 00:01:20,742
It names the program that reads the file.

33
00:01:20,742 --> 00:01:24,190
Run the script by naming that program yourself.

34
00:01:24,190 --> 00:01:25,437
Type: bash hi.sh.

35
00:01:25,987 --> 00:01:26,659
It works.

36
00:01:26,659 --> 00:01:30,318
Dollar zero holds the name the script was run as.

37
00:01:30,318 --> 00:01:31,811
Now run it directly,

38
00:01:31,811 --> 00:01:33,678
the way a real tool runs.

39
00:01:33,678 --> 00:01:35,694
Type: dot slash hi dot S H.

40
00:01:36,244 --> 00:01:37,832
Permission denied.

41
00:01:37,832 --> 00:01:42,507
A file is not a program until it has the execute bit.

42
00:01:42,507 --> 00:01:44,095
You know this fix.

43
00:01:44,095 --> 00:01:46,036
Type: chmod u+x hi.sh.

44
00:01:46,586 --> 00:01:50,319
Now run it again. Type: dot slash hi dot S H.

45
00:01:50,869 --> 00:01:51,429
It runs,

46
00:01:51,429 --> 00:01:54,649
and dollar zero now says dot slash hi dot S H.

47
00:01:54,649 --> 00:01:58,358
The shebang told the system to hand the file to bash.

48
00:01:58,358 --> 00:01:59,688
Two ways to run it,

49
00:01:59,688 --> 00:02:00,458
one result,

50
00:02:00,458 --> 00:02:02,837
as long as the file is executable.

51
00:02:04,887 --> 00:02:05,873
Next: quoting.

52
00:02:05,873 --> 00:02:09,677
Quotes tell the shell which characters are plain text.

53
00:02:09,677 --> 00:02:10,100
First,

54
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:12,001
store a name in a variable.

55
00:02:12,001 --> 00:02:13,058
Type: name=Sam.

56
00:02:13,058 --> 00:02:16,087
There are no spaces around the equals sign.

57
00:02:16,637 --> 00:02:17,725
Nothing printed,

58
00:02:17,725 --> 00:02:19,016
and that is normal.

59
00:02:19,016 --> 00:02:21,871
Now use the variable inside double quotes.

60
00:02:21,871 --> 00:02:22,618
Type: echo,

61
00:02:22,618 --> 00:02:23,502
double quote,

62
00:02:23,502 --> 00:02:24,521
Hi dollar name,

63
00:02:24,521 --> 00:02:25,405
double quote.

64
00:02:25,955 --> 00:02:28,669
Double quotes let the variable expand.

65
00:02:28,669 --> 00:02:29,169
Hi Sam.

66
00:02:29,169 --> 00:02:30,454
Now single quotes.

67
00:02:30,454 --> 00:02:31,240
Type: echo,

68
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:32,169
single quote,

69
00:02:32,169 --> 00:02:33,240
Hi dollar name,

70
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:34,168
single quote.

71
00:02:34,718 --> 00:02:37,772
Single quotes keep every character literal,

72
00:02:37,772 --> 00:02:39,548
so the dollar sign stays.

73
00:02:39,548 --> 00:02:41,465
Rule: double quotes expand,

74
00:02:41,465 --> 00:02:42,957
single quotes do not.

75
00:02:42,957 --> 00:02:44,306
Now a classic trap.

76
00:02:44,306 --> 00:02:45,514
List this folder.

77
00:02:45,514 --> 00:02:46,153
Type: ls.

78
00:02:46,703 --> 00:02:47,410
One file,

79
00:02:47,410 --> 00:02:49,374
with a space in its name.

80
00:02:49,374 --> 00:02:51,887
Try to remove it without quotes.

81
00:02:51,887 --> 00:02:53,615
Type: rm my notes.txt.

82
00:02:54,165 --> 00:02:54,937
Two errors.

83
00:02:54,937 --> 00:02:55,989
Without quotes,

84
00:02:55,989 --> 00:02:58,795
the shell split the name into two words,

85
00:02:58,795 --> 00:03:02,022
and rm looked for two files that do not exist.

86
00:03:02,022 --> 00:03:03,075
Quote the name.

87
00:03:03,075 --> 00:03:03,706
Type: rm,

88
00:03:03,706 --> 00:03:04,618
double quote,

89
00:03:04,618 --> 00:03:05,881
my notes dot text,

90
00:03:05,881 --> 00:03:06,793
double quote,

91
00:03:06,793 --> 00:03:07,354
and and,

92
00:03:07,354 --> 00:03:08,266
echo removed.

93
00:03:08,816 --> 00:03:12,422
Removed. Quote your variables and your file names, every time.

94
00:03:14,372 --> 00:03:15,330
Now expansion.

95
00:03:15,330 --> 00:03:19,575
The shell rewrites parts of your line before the command runs.

96
00:03:19,575 --> 00:03:21,013
Start with the tilde.

97
00:03:21,013 --> 00:03:21,903
Type: echo ~.

98
00:03:22,453 --> 00:03:24,795
The tilde became your home folder.

99
00:03:24,795 --> 00:03:26,379
Echo never saw a tilde.

100
00:03:26,379 --> 00:03:26,724
Next,

101
00:03:26,724 --> 00:03:31,270
command substitution: a dollar sign and brackets around a command.

102
00:03:31,270 --> 00:03:32,855
The output replaces it.

103
00:03:32,855 --> 00:03:33,613
Type: echo,

104
00:03:33,613 --> 00:03:34,508
double quote,

105
00:03:34,508 --> 00:03:35,128
today is,

106
00:03:35,128 --> 00:03:35,610
dollar,

107
00:03:35,610 --> 00:03:36,162
bracket,

108
00:03:36,162 --> 00:03:37,539
date plus percent A,

109
00:03:37,539 --> 00:03:38,090
bracket,

110
00:03:38,090 --> 00:03:38,986
double quote.

111
00:03:39,536 --> 00:03:41,503
The date command ran first,

112
00:03:41,503 --> 00:03:44,417
and its output was pasted into the line.

113
00:03:44,417 --> 00:03:45,437
Now wildcards.

114
00:03:45,437 --> 00:03:47,259
Type: echo star dot text.

115
00:03:47,809 --> 00:03:52,480
The shell expanded the star into the matching file names.

116
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:55,430
Echo just printed what it was given.

117
00:03:55,430 --> 00:03:56,331
Now braces,

118
00:03:56,331 --> 00:03:57,888
which build a list.

119
00:03:57,888 --> 00:03:59,609
Type: echo file dash,

120
00:03:59,609 --> 00:04:00,264
brace a,

121
00:04:00,264 --> 00:04:00,428
b,

122
00:04:00,428 --> 00:04:00,592
c,

123
00:04:00,592 --> 00:04:01,084
brace,

124
00:04:01,084 --> 00:04:01,739
dot log.

125
00:04:02,289 --> 00:04:03,933
Braces made three words.

126
00:04:03,933 --> 00:04:05,372
One more: arithmetic,

127
00:04:05,372 --> 00:04:07,701
with a dollar and double brackets.

128
00:04:07,701 --> 00:04:08,454
Type: echo,

129
00:04:08,454 --> 00:04:08,934
dollar,

130
00:04:08,934 --> 00:04:09,961
double bracket,

131
00:04:09,961 --> 00:04:11,057
six times seven.

132
00:04:11,607 --> 00:04:12,258
Forty two.

133
00:04:12,258 --> 00:04:12,844
Remember,

134
00:04:12,844 --> 00:04:14,406
expansion happens first,

135
00:04:14,406 --> 00:04:16,099
and then the command runs.

136
00:04:16,099 --> 00:04:18,637
If you want text kept exactly as typed,

137
00:04:18,637 --> 00:04:19,223
quote it.

138
00:04:21,273 --> 00:04:21,635
Next,

139
00:04:21,635 --> 00:04:22,792
the environment.

140
00:04:22,792 --> 00:04:25,973
Every program carries a set of named values.

141
00:04:25,973 --> 00:04:29,589
A plain shell variable belongs only to this shell.

142
00:04:29,589 --> 00:04:31,108
Type: GREETING=hello.

143
00:04:31,658 --> 00:04:33,194
Now start a child shell,

144
00:04:33,194 --> 00:04:35,306
and ask it to print the variable.

145
00:04:35,306 --> 00:04:36,458
Type: bash dash c,

146
00:04:36,458 --> 00:04:38,634
then the command in single quotes.

147
00:04:39,184 --> 00:04:39,584
Empty.

148
00:04:39,584 --> 00:04:41,185
The child cannot see it.

149
00:04:41,185 --> 00:04:43,854
To share a variable with child programs,

150
00:04:43,854 --> 00:04:44,521
export it.

151
00:04:44,521 --> 00:04:45,989
Type: export GREETING.

152
00:04:46,539 --> 00:04:51,254
Now run the same test again. Type: bash dash c, and the same command.

153
00:04:51,804 --> 00:04:53,638
Now the child sees hello.

154
00:04:53,638 --> 00:04:56,500
Exported variables are the environment,

155
00:04:56,500 --> 00:04:58,481
passed down to every child.

156
00:04:58,481 --> 00:05:00,463
One famous example is path.

157
00:05:00,463 --> 00:05:02,150
Type: echo dollar PATH.

158
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:03,571
These folders,

159
00:05:03,571 --> 00:05:04,815
separated by colons,

160
00:05:04,815 --> 00:05:07,241
are where the shell looks for commands,

161
00:05:07,241 --> 00:05:07,800
in order.

162
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:09,977
That is why a command can be found,

163
00:05:09,977 --> 00:05:10,786
or not found.

164
00:05:12,836 --> 00:05:14,882
Last idea: exit status.

165
00:05:14,882 --> 00:05:17,818
Every command ends with a number.

166
00:05:17,818 --> 00:05:18,351
First,

167
00:05:18,351 --> 00:05:20,219
a command that works.

168
00:05:20,219 --> 00:05:22,265
Type: ls /etc/hostname.

169
00:05:22,815 --> 00:05:24,415
It printed the file name.

170
00:05:24,415 --> 00:05:25,375
Did it succeed?

171
00:05:25,375 --> 00:05:28,639
The shell keeps the answer in dollar question mark.

172
00:05:28,639 --> 00:05:30,687
Type: echo dollar question mark.

173
00:05:31,237 --> 00:05:36,763
Zero. Zero means success. Now a command that fails. Type: ls /nope.

174
00:05:37,313 --> 00:05:42,902
An error message. But the number is the real signal. Type: echo dollar question mark.

175
00:05:43,452 --> 00:05:43,757
Two.

176
00:05:43,757 --> 00:05:46,199
Anything but zero means failure,

177
00:05:46,199 --> 00:05:48,259
and the number can say why.

178
00:05:48,259 --> 00:05:50,548
Scripts use this with and and,

179
00:05:50,548 --> 00:05:51,311
and or or.

180
00:05:51,311 --> 00:05:54,973
Type: grep dash q root slash E T C slash passwd,

181
00:05:54,973 --> 00:05:55,583
and and,

182
00:05:55,583 --> 00:05:56,423
echo found.

183
00:05:56,973 --> 00:05:57,426
Found.

184
00:05:57,426 --> 00:06:02,115
And and runs the next command only if the first one succeeded.

185
00:06:02,115 --> 00:06:03,325
Now the reverse.

186
00:06:03,325 --> 00:06:04,686
Type: grep dash q,

187
00:06:04,686 --> 00:06:05,064
zzzz,

188
00:06:05,064 --> 00:06:06,123
the same file,

189
00:06:06,123 --> 00:06:06,955
then or or,

190
00:06:06,955 --> 00:06:07,938
echo missing.

191
00:06:08,488 --> 00:06:09,012
Missing.

192
00:06:09,012 --> 00:06:12,748
Or or runs the next command only if the first one failed.

193
00:06:12,748 --> 00:06:13,796
Zero is success,

194
00:06:13,796 --> 00:06:15,435
anything else is failure.

195
00:06:15,435 --> 00:06:18,515
Scripts decide what to do from this one number.

196
00:06:20,565 --> 00:06:21,888
Four myths to clear up.

197
00:06:22,438 --> 00:06:25,113
Myth one: a script must end in dot S H.

198
00:06:25,113 --> 00:06:25,319
No.

199
00:06:25,319 --> 00:06:27,034
The name does not matter.

200
00:06:27,034 --> 00:06:29,435
The shebang and the execute bit do.

201
00:06:29,985 --> 00:06:33,108
Myth two: single and double quotes are the same.

202
00:06:33,108 --> 00:06:33,303
No.

203
00:06:33,303 --> 00:06:35,320
Double quotes expand variables.

204
00:06:35,320 --> 00:06:37,793
Single quotes keep everything literal.

205
00:06:38,343 --> 00:06:43,420
Myth three: zero means false. In the shell it is the opposite. Zero means success.

206
00:06:43,970 --> 00:06:46,832
Myth four: quotes are optional if it works today.

207
00:06:46,832 --> 00:06:49,635
A file name with a space will break it tomorrow.

208
00:06:49,635 --> 00:06:50,861
Quote your variables.

209
00:06:51,411 --> 00:06:54,078
Quote it. Expand it. Check the exit status.

210
00:06:56,128 --> 00:07:00,693
Here is how you check your own understanding. Six points, and you need all six.

211
00:07:01,243 --> 00:07:05,382
Run a script two ways, with bash and directly, and say what the shebang does.

212
00:07:05,932 --> 00:07:08,705
Say when to use single quotes, and when to use double.

213
00:07:09,255 --> 00:07:14,397
Name four expansions: tilde, variables, command substitution, and wildcards.

214
00:07:14,947 --> 00:07:19,640
Explain why a child does not see an unexported variable, and fix it with export.

215
00:07:20,190 --> 00:07:24,691
Read an exit status with dollar question mark, and use and and, or or.

216
00:07:25,241 --> 00:07:28,164
Quote a file name that has a space, and explain why.

217
00:07:28,714 --> 00:07:30,335
To pass, you need all six.

218
00:07:32,385 --> 00:07:33,037
Your turn,

219
00:07:33,037 --> 00:07:35,317
in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

220
00:07:35,317 --> 00:07:36,295
Take your time,

221
00:07:36,295 --> 00:07:38,380
and type every command yourself.

222
00:07:38,930 --> 00:07:41,699
One: write a three line script with a shebang.

223
00:07:41,699 --> 00:07:42,723
Run it with bash,

224
00:07:42,723 --> 00:07:45,372
then make it executable and run it directly.

225
00:07:45,922 --> 00:07:48,563
Two: create a file with a space in its name.

226
00:07:48,563 --> 00:07:50,484
Try to remove it without quotes,

227
00:07:50,484 --> 00:07:51,384
read the error,

228
00:07:51,384 --> 00:07:53,005
then remove it with quotes.

229
00:07:53,555 --> 00:07:55,983
Three: print a message with single quotes,

230
00:07:55,983 --> 00:07:57,660
and again with double quotes,

231
00:07:57,660 --> 00:07:58,643
using a variable.

232
00:07:58,643 --> 00:07:59,742
Compare the output.

233
00:08:00,292 --> 00:08:03,472
Four: use command substitution to print today's date,

234
00:08:03,472 --> 00:08:06,052
and a brace list to print three file names.

235
00:08:06,602 --> 00:08:10,932
Five: set a variable, test it in a child shell, export it, and test again.

236
00:08:11,482 --> 00:08:17,114
Six: run one command that succeeds, and one that fails, and print the exit status of each.

237
00:08:17,664 --> 00:08:20,971
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

238
00:08:36,521 --> 00:08:37,396
Quick check.

239
00:08:37,946 --> 00:08:39,439
What does the shebang line do?

240
00:08:39,989 --> 00:08:42,890
Which quotes expand a variable, single or double?

241
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:45,638
What does an exit status of zero mean?

242
00:08:46,188 --> 00:08:47,937
Why must you export a variable?

243
00:08:48,487 --> 00:08:54,183
Objective covered: MC05-O01.

244
00:08:54,733 --> 00:08:56,802
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

245
00:09:07,352 --> 00:09:11,832
Next lesson: variables, arguments, input and command substitution.

246
00:09:12,382 --> 00:09:15,710
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
